  <Document>
    <name>🌞 LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</h2><p><b>Data time:</b> 2026-09-25 02:00 AM EDT<br/><b>Source updated:</b> n/a (NOMADS)<br/><b>Source version:</b> gfs-20260925-t06z-f000<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-25 06:25 AM EDT<br/><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT MODEL (analysis)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/solar_radiation/legend.png?v=gfs-20260925-t06z-f000" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Surface downward shortwave solar radiation (W/m^2, GFS f000 analysis step), FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;1000+: night <b>0</b> (deep blue) &rarr; low <b>150</b> (green) &rarr; moderate <b>300&ndash;450</b> (yellow/amber) &rarr; high <b>600</b> (orange-red) &rarr; very high <b>750</b> (red) &rarr; extreme <b>1000+</b> (violet). <b>LOWEST 0</b>, <b>HIGHEST+ 1000+</b>. Same flux always shows the same color. Values shown exactly as observed; night reads zero; clouds reduce values quantitatively. Broadband flux display bands &mdash; not the EPA UV Index (separate product).</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP GFS operational surface analysis, DSWRF (downward short-wave radiation flux), 6-hourly cycles (00/06/12/18Z)<br/><a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/gfs/prod/">https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/gfs/prod/</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h2>LIVE SOLAR RADIATION</h2><p>Surface downward shortwave solar radiation flux: solar power arriving at the surface, GFS f000 analysis step for the newest available cycle. Nighttime reads zero (valid data, deep blue) — not missing data. Clouds reduce values quantitatively versus clear sky.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/solar_radiation/legend.png?v=gfs-20260925-t06z-f000" width="600" alt="key"></p><p>Surface downward shortwave solar radiation (W/m^2, GFS f000 analysis step), FIXED absolute scale 0&ndash;1000+: night <b>0</b> (deep blue) &rarr; low <b>150</b> (green) &rarr; moderate <b>300&ndash;450</b> (yellow/amber) &rarr; high <b>600</b> (orange-red) &rarr; very high <b>750</b> (red) &rarr; extreme <b>1000+</b> (violet). <b>LOWEST 0</b>, <b>HIGHEST+ 1000+</b>. Same flux always shows the same color. Values shown exactly as observed; night reads zero; clouds reduce values quantitatively. Broadband flux display bands &mdash; not the EPA UV Index (separate product).</p><p><b>Variable:</b> DSWRF surface f000 analysis step (newest available GFS cycle)<br/><b>Units:</b> W/m^2 (f000 analysis-step flux of the newest available GFS cycle (not instantaneous, not daily accumulation))<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA/NCEP GFS operational surface analysis, DSWRF (downward short-wave radiation flux), 6-hourly cycles (00/06/12/18Z)<br/><b>Update:</b> GFS 6-hourly analysis cycles (00/06/12/18Z)<br/><b>Data time:</b> 2026-09-25 02:00 AM EDT<br/><b>Source version:</b> gfs-20260925-t06z-f000<br/><b>Processed:</b> 2026-09-25 06:25 AM EDT<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://nomads.ncep.noaa.gov/pub/data/nccf/com/gfs/prod/">NOMADS GFS</a></p>]]></description>
